<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jiang Guangnai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1888–1967</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jiang Guangnai was a Chinese general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, commander of the Nineteenth Route Army, which resisted the Japanese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in January–March 1932. Transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-tingkai">Cai Tingkai</a> led the Fujian Rebellion of November 1933 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, which was crushed early in 1934. He later joined the Revolutionary Committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomintang">Kuomintang</a> and served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> as minister of the textile industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiang-guangnai.jpg" alt="Jiang Guangnai" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jiang Guangnai · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jiang_Guangnai.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
